$NetBSD$

Upstream: https://git.kernel.org/pub/scm/linux/kernel/git/torvalds/linux.git
Mirror: https://github.com/torvalds/linux
Tag: v6.12
Commit: adc218676eef25575469234709c2d87185ca223a
Import date: 2026-10-31
License: GPL-2.0 or GPL-2.0+, as specified in each imported file.

This is a pinned source subset for the initial metadata-reading milestones.
v6.12 supplies both v4 and v5 disk definitions and a reproducible
baseline; this is not a claim to track the latest Linux XFS release.

Pristine files in dist/ come from fs/xfs/libxfs/ at that commit:

    xfs_format.h
    xfs_log_format.h
    xfs_sb.c
    xfs_sb.h
    xfs_types.h
    xfs_alloc.c
    xfs_ialloc.c
    xfs_cksum.h
    xfs_btree.c
    xfs_rmap_btree.c
    xfs_refcount_btree.c
    xfs_inode_buf.c
    xfs_inode_fork.c
    xfs_ialloc_btree.c
    xfs_bmap_btree.c
    xfs_da_format.h
    xfs_da_btree.c
    xfs_dir2.c
    xfs_dir2_priv.h
    xfs_dir2_sf.c
    xfs_dir2_data.c
    xfs_symlink_remote.c
    xfs_attr_leaf.c
    xfs_attr_remote.c

dist/xfs_dir2_readdir.c, dist/xfs_log.c and dist/xfs_log_recover.c come from
fs/xfs/ (outside libxfs/) at that commit.

COPYING is the unmodified LICENSES/preferred/GPL-2.0 file from that commit.
SHA256 records the hashes of these twenty-eight upstream files. Verify them with
"sha256sum -c SHA256" from this directory (or the equivalent sha256 tool).
Do not edit dist/ for NetBSD adaptations.

netbsd/xfs_sb.c selects these routines from dist/xfs_sb.c without changing
their bodies:

    xfs_sb_validate_v5_features
    xfs_sb_good_version
    xfs_sb_quota_from_disk
    __xfs_sb_from_disk
    xfs_sb_from_disk

include/xfs_quota_flags.h selects the quota flag block from
dist/xfs_log_format.h, from XFS_UQUOTA_ACCT through XFS_PQUOTA_CHKD.
Their original copyright notices and GPL identifiers are retained.

include/xfs_compat.h supplies the scalar types, endian conversions, assertions
and UUID-copy operation used by these routines. Linux's opaque UUID bytes
remain distinct from NetBSD's structured UUID representation.

netbsd/xfs_cksum.c adapts xfs_start_cksum_safe / xfs_verify_cksum from
dist/xfs_cksum.h: the field offset is checked, unaligned checksum loads use
memcpy, and a software Castagnoli CRC replaces Linux's crc32c service.
No checksum-generation/write path is exposed.
The internal xfs_crc32c helper also feeds the noncontiguous journal checksum.

include/xfs_log_layout.h selects the physical record and operation layouts
from xfs_log_format.h, using the port UUID type. netbsd/xfs_read_log.c adapts
cycle-stamp interpretation, head/record positioning, xlog_check_unmount_rec
and packed cycle-data restoration from xfs_log_recover.c, and xlog_cksum /
the unmount operation format from xfs_log.c. Log size/sector/stripe checks
follow xfs_sb.c and the pinned xfs_fs.h limits. These adaptations deliberately:

  - scan every cycle stamp rather than use the recovery search heuristics;
  - reject nonmonotonic cycles, rollover and incomplete latest records,
    without trimming to an older record, replaying or clearing stale blocks;
  - bound sizes, require the record to end exactly at the head, verify its
    UUID/LSNs/cycles and checksum, and restore cycle data only afterward;
  - require exactly one well-formed modern eight-byte unmount operation to
    report CLEAN, with explicit little/big-endian payload decoding;
  - handle the special unchecksummed mkfs record only at cycle 1/block 0,
    with canonical fields/padding and a verified wholly zero log suffix;
  - return NEEDS_RECOVERY for a checksummed latest record without an unmount,
    and errors for unsupported/malformed journals, preserving output on error.

The checksum covers the used extended-header regions, not entire 512-byte
header sectors. Both upstream header ABI sizes (324 and 328 bytes) are
accepted. No transaction items are interpreted or replayed. Cleanliness
does not imply global consistency, and metadata LSNs are not yet checked
against the log. The caller must keep the entire image quiescent.

netbsd/xfs_ag.c adapts the following read-side checks to an explicit reader
context, without creating placeholder Linux mount, buffer, or log objects:

    xfs_agf_verify, xfs_agfl_verify, xfs_validate_ag_length,
    xfs_agfl_needs_reset (xfs_alloc.c)
    xfs_agi_verify (xfs_ialloc.c)
    xfs_btree_compute_maxlevels, xfs_btree_space_to_height (xfs_btree.c)
    xfs_rmapbt_compute_maxlevels (xfs_rmap_btree.c)
    xfs_refcountbt_compute_maxlevels (xfs_refcount_btree.c)

Unlike the unchanged superblock routines, these adaptations intentionally:

  - return positive errno values and static diagnostics;
  - require the exact AG length calculated from the primary superblock,
    including the shortened last AG; concurrent growfs is unsupported;
  - check tree-root block bounds, inode counters and active free-list slots;
  - reject an inconsistent free-list ring instead of repairing/resetting it;
  - check inactive v5 slots against the actual AG length, but ignore unused
    v4 slots, which historical mkfs versions left uninitialized;
  - interpret unlinked inode pointers as AG-relative inode numbers;
  - do not call xfs_log_check_lsn: no log has been initialized or recovered.

Tree-depth limits use upstream record sizes and fanout calculations, including
the distinct reverse-map bound for reflink filesystems. The 64-block minimum
AG size is XFS_MIN_AG_BLOCKS from libxfs/xfs_fs.h at the same pinned commit.
These verifiers do not establish journal cleanliness or tree consistency.

netbsd/xfs_read_inode.c adapts selected xfs_dinode_verify, fork/forkoff,
nrext64 and extent-size hint checks from xfs_inode_buf.c, fork geometry
checks from xfs_inode_fork.c, and xfs_imap_lookup / xfs_inobt_check_irec
allocation checks from xfs_ialloc.c. Sparse holemask expansion follows
xfs_inobt_irec_to_allocmask from xfs_ialloc_btree.c. The port always checks
allocation, including for image-supplied inode numbers. It copies disk data
to aligned structures, verifies v3 CRCs before trusting inode identity, and
publishes a caller-owned inode image only after the selected checks pass.
Inode-tree leaf records and interior keys use within-block chunk alignment,
as in xfs_dialloc's XFS_AGINO_TO_OFFSET assertions; the 64-inode record width
does not imply 64-inode start alignment on legacy layouts.

netbsd/xfs_inode_stat.c adapts xfs_inode_from_disk and xfs_inode_from_disk_ts
from xfs_inode_buf.c. It decodes ownership, v1/v2 link counts, allocation,
generation, flags, changecount and legacy/bigtime timestamps into portable
values. It rejects nanoseconds outside [0, 1000000000), bigtime seconds past
XFS_BIGTIME_TIME_MAX and allocated byte counts exceeding INT64_MAX. It
reports the presence of an attribute fork without interpreting its contents.

netbsd/xfs_read_attr.c adapts xfs_attr_shortform_verify from xfs_attr_leaf.c,
using the packed header/entry layout and namespace flags from xfs_da_format.h.
It bounds the declared total against the inode fork before traversing entries,
checks the exact count/size, lengths, namespace combinations, and forbidden
local/incomplete bits. The unused header padding is ignored as upstream does.
It additionally rejects embedded NUL names and duplicate byte names within
each namespace. All structural checks precede callbacks, including corruption
in later entries; iteration uses bounded stack offsets and allocates nothing.
Empty retained extent forks are accepted. Parent-pointer attributes remain
unsupported. Parent-pointer duplicate-name
semantics are not treated as ordinary xattr semantics.

netbsd/xfs_read_attr_external.c adapts the read-side leaf verification, node
traversal and remote-value decoding from xfs_attr_leaf.c, xfs_da_btree.c and
xfs_attr_remote.c. include/xfs_attr_layout.h selects their on-disk layouts
from xfs_da_format.h. The shared bmap reader now accepts attribute forks with
32-bit logical block addresses. Attribute mappings must match the inode's
extent count; holes are allowed between objects, unwritten mappings are not.
Mapped blocks, indexes and a 64 KiB value staging area occupy caller-owned
workspace (8 MiB in mount_xfs). No allocator or Linux buffer cache is emulated.

The reader verifies all mapped attribute blocks before callbacks. It checks
tree depth, parent hashes, exact sibling chains, namespace/name/hash validity,
record and free-map bounds/overlaps, used-byte counts, duplicate names and
remote-value coverage. Leaf firstused may lag when holes are present, as in
upstream split/move operations. It preserves the historical remote-name size
round_up(11 + namelen, 4). On v5 it checks full-block CRC32C, metadata UUID,
owner and physical address for tree and remote blocks, plus exact remote
offsets/lengths. Remote headers occupy 56 bytes per filesystem block. V4
remote blocks have no headers. Values may be up to 65536 bytes.

Incomplete entries, parent pointers, shared/aliased attribute data blocks,
unreferenced mapped blocks and missing remote blocks are rejected. Sorted
name indexes detect duplicates without quadratic hash-collision scans.
Pointers passed to callbacks are valid only during that callback; a remote
value reuses staging space. Enumeration order is unspecified. The checks do
not establish global allocation ownership or validate LSNs against the log.

The separate xfs_attrs_check_readonly policy accepts user attributes and only
the root directory's trusted xfs:autofsck property with values none, check,
optimize or repair. It rejects ACLs, security attributes, unknown trusted
attributes, unknown property values, and the same property on other inodes.
The property is exposed as metadata; it triggers no scanning or repair.
Its layout/meaning follows the XFS filesystem-property documentation:
https://kernel.googlesource.com/pub/scm/fs/xfs/xfs-documentation/+/master/design/XFS_Filesystem_Structure/fs_properties.asciidoc

netbsd/xfs_read_btree.c adapts xfs_btree_{agblock,fsblock}_v5hdr_verify,
xfs_btree_{agblock,fsblock}_verify, xfs_inobt_verify and xfs_bmbt_verify.
The inode-tree and bmap traversals require exact owners and disk addresses,
check every key and pointer in a visited node, enforce decreasing levels,
and carry parent key intervals down to the child. Sibling addresses are
bounded, but reciprocal links and unvisited subtrees are not verified.
All inode/data/tree addresses exclude the AG headers and internal log.

netbsd/xfs_read_file.c adapts xfs_bmbt_disk_get_all's packed extent decoding
and bmap root layout from xfs_bmap_btree.c. Fork validation follows
xfs_iformat_extents / xfs_iformat_btree from xfs_inode_fork.c. The bmap
height limit conservatively allows a single root pointer over external
nodes at minimum fanout, up to the maximum large data-fork extent count.
Selected leaves are checked for order, overlap, parent range and physical
bounds. Holes and unwritten extents return zeros without reading their
backing data. File reads use caller-owned block scratch, clamp at EOF and
report zero bytes completed on failure (the destination may be altered).

include/xfs_dir_layout.h selects directory constants and structures from
xfs_da_format.h, replacing uuid_t with the port's opaque UUID type. It does
not import Linux-only accessor/attribute helpers into the compatibility layer.

netbsd/xfs_read_directory.c adapts xfs_dir2_sf_verify and the shortform inode
number/entry accessors, __xfs_dir3_data_check, xfs_dir3_data_verify and
xfs_dir2_data_freefind_verify. Geometry and entry sizes follow xfs_dir2.c and
xfs_dir2_priv.h. Name hashing uses xfs_da_hashname's rotate/XOR operation.
The linear scan follows xfs_dir2_{sf,block,leaf}_getdents in xfs_dir2_readdir.c;
leaf and node directories both enumerate their data blocks. The port also:

  - bounds all variable-size fields before reading them, including unaligned
    shortform inode numbers and tags;
  - checks exact owner/address/UUID, directory dots and root parent, file
    types, names, free-entry tags and the three largest free areas;
  - uses a bitset to verify a bijection between live data entries and the
    single-block directory's embedded hash index (including hash collisions);
  - assembles directory blocks from possibly fragmented filesystem blocks;
  - skips whole aligned holes, rejecting unwritten or partially missing data;
  - implements exact-name lookup by scanning all entries, rejecting duplicate
    matches and preserving the result when a later block fails verification.

External leaf/node hash indexes and free-space indexes are not read or
verified. This deliberately uses the data-only readdir path for inspection
lookup, not upstream's hash-index lookup algorithm. It cannot establish
agreement with those indexes. ASCII case-insensitive directories are rejected.
Directory callbacks see only entries from fully verified blocks, but callbacks
from preceding blocks cannot be rolled back after a later error.

netbsd/xfs_read_symlink.c adapts xfs_symlink_remote_read, xfs_symlink_verify
and xfs_symlink_hdr_ok. It verifies complete mapped buffers (one header per
extent, not per filesystem block), target offsets/lengths, exact inode owner,
disk address, UUID, CRC and absence of embedded NULs. Targets must be shorter
than XFS_SYMLINK_MAXLEN. Even a partial read checks every target chunk, rejecting
holes and unwritten mappings. A contiguous two-block target on a v5 filesystem
with 1 KiB blocks requires 2 KiB of scratch. Metadata LSN checks remain absent.

netbsd/xfs_lookup_path.c is a new image-only adapter around these readers.
It resolves absolute paths, dots and relative/absolute symlinks with caller
workspace, a 4096-byte path bound and a 40-link limit. It checks allocation
when opening every selected inode, preserves trailing-slash semantics and
never passes image path components to the host filesystem. It does not
implement credentials, access control or NetBSD namei/VFS integration.

These readers are image-inspection adaptations, not a replacement for all
live-kernel verifiers or xfs_repair. In particular they do not cross-check
global extent/block counts, rmap ownership, other allocation trees, inode
timestamp consistency, external directory indexes, or log LSNs.
Inline and external attributes are verified separately by xfs_read_attrs;
xfs_read_inode remains a structural inode/data-fork reader.
Bmap blocks are verified lazily during file reads. Unknown read-only-compatible features
block inode access even though the superblock probe permits inspection.
Zero-length directories/symlinks are explicitly unsupported.
No Linux cache, locking, transaction or recovery object is
emulated; those contracts remain work for future caching/recovery milestones.

puffs/mount_xfs.c is a new direct libpuffs adapter. It checks journal
cleanliness before mounting and applies the complete attribute mount policy
before publishing each node, so unsupported access-control metadata cannot be
silently ignored. All engine I/O stays in user space
through a read-only regular-file descriptor. The adapter supplies credentials,
stable inode cookies, vattr translation, directory cookies and read-only
operation/error handling using NetBSD's public libpuffs API. getextattr and
listextattr expose raw names through NetBSD's user/system namespaces, checking
file read permissions for user attributes and privileged credentials for
system attributes. There is no Linux-style name-prefix translation.
The accompanying BSD-licensed lib/libpuffs/dispatcher.c change reserves
reply capacity for GETEXTATTR/LISTEXTATTR, matching READ/READDIR. Without it,
large attribute callbacks can overrun the received header-sized frame. This
is a libpuffs allocation fix; no GPL code is incorporated into libpuffs.

The wrapper, compatibility definitions, tests and scripts are GPL-2.0-only.
No SGI/Linux code has been relicensed as BSD code. This directory is not wired
into the installed header set or default base-system build. It now lives in
external/gpl2/xfs for a user-space PUFFS/libpuffs server; the earlier location
was sys/external/gpl2/xfs. Moving the code does not change its license.

For a future import, update the commit and original files together, review
changes to the selected routines and compatibility layer, regenerate SHA256,
and rerun all tests before accepting any new feature bits.
